WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.110 align:start position:0%
 
this is georgetown capital of british  guiana on the northern coast of south  america  it is populated by the descendants of  african east indian portuguese chinese  europeans and south american indians  the commercial center of georgetown lies  close to the waterfront  here are modern buildings department  stores and supermarkets  there are also churches  and cinemas  the coastal railroad parallels the  coastal highway running eastward from  georgetown  another paved highway runs 25 miles  south to atkinson airfield  british guyana has an agricultural  economy chiefly sugar and rice  the industrious viennese tend their  farms well  this is a native rice plantation  fishing is popular and productive and  helps to vary the diet  in the american indian villages women  make bread and many dishes from cassava  their staple food  local high schools prepare students for  university entrance and a national  university is being established  the people of british guiana are great  sports enthusiasts  if these people were deprived of the use  of their limbs they would not only be  denied much pleasure but they could no  longer tend their farms or make a living  that is why it is so important that they  understand british guiana's crippling  disease salaria  malaria or filariasis is carried by  mosquitoes and may lead to swelling of  the legs arms and other parts of the  body after years of repeated infection  the disease is more often seen in  females because their legs are exposed  before a swelling appears the blood of  the infected person contains young  embryo worms called microfilari  these microfilarii can be seen in the  infected person's blood only at night  when a female filaria mosquito feeds on  infected person's blood it sucks up  these microfilari which then pass into  the stomach of the mosquito  in the mosquito stomach the micropolaria  remains active for a short while then  bores through the wall of the stomach  and moves to the thorax or chest region  of the mosquito  in a muscle fiber of the thorax the  young larva grows short and thick until  it becomes shaped like a sausage  then it enters into the second stage and  gradually grows longer and narrower  until it becomes an infective larva  it wriggles its way from the thorax to  the head of the mosquito and enters the  proboscis or beak  when the infected mosquito bends down to  bite again the infective larva crawls  out of the proboscis and drops onto the  skin of the person being bitten  then it goes into the skin and travels  around the person's body  in about a year it becomes a sexually  mature male or female worm  the worms mate and the eggs develop into  a microfilarii which appear in the  person's blood  thus completing the cycle  one of the main methods of detecting  whether one has malaria before the  swelling appears is by making a blood  smear  blood is taken from a finger at night  early in the morning the microfilaria go  deep into the body  if there is at least one microfilaria  present the person is said to have  positive blood  if his blood is negative it does not  necessarily mean that he is free of  infection  this is because the worms may have  entered the person's body recently and  are not sure enough to produce the micro  polarity  also when a person already has a  swelling his blood is usually negative  there are many effects of filaria  long-standing infection may lead to  swelling of the limbs called  elephantiasis  at first tissue fluid or lymph  accumulates but later there are  permanent changes in the tissues  themselves  the disease cripples physically mentally  economically and socially  the most damaging effect is felt by the  wage earner and his family  a job is not readily given to a person  who has elephantiasis if he has to stay  on his feet all day long  young girls having elephantiasis usually  are denied romance and marriage because  of their permanently swollen limbs  the depressive state which this disease  leads to is one of its most damaging  effects  if he does have a job he has to carry  out his duties the best he can even with  his legs swollen  convinced that something could be done  about the valeria problem the  governments of british guiana and the  united states of america have been  working together to solve it  on april 29 1961 representatives of the  ministry of health and the united states  agency for international development  agreed to finance a joint solarisis  research and control project this  project comes under the technical  assistance development organization  abbreviated as tado  the united states government has  supplied money equipment and three  experts  a medical entomologist who studies the  mosquitoes in order to determine which  ones transmit filariasis from person to  person  a parasitologist who studies the  parasites or worms which cause the  disease  and a health educator who informs the  public concerning the disease and  secures their cooperation while control  measures are being undertaken  the ministry of health has long worked  to control philosophy  in 1955 an energetic doctor began a  countrywide campaign to control disease  this campaign was carried out for about  six years prior to the present joint  program  in the filariasis clinics d's doctors  studied increase or decrease in size of  limbs by displacement of water  the patient steps into a water-filled  drum  water comes out of the spout and is  measured  blood tests are also taken at night in  the filariasis clinics so that the  patient's case history will be complete  clinics are held not only in georgetown  but also in the coastal villages having  much filariasis  in the headquarters building of the  filariasis research and control unit  there are offices and laboratories for  entomology parasitology clinical and  health education sections  the united states medical entomologist  has trained viennese personnel in many  aspects of etymological work with  valeria mosquitoes both in the field and  the laboratory  the united states parasitologist has  trained guianese in taking blood smears  and identification of filaria parasites  found in the smears  a typical filarial area along the east  coast including buxton and surroundings  was selected as the first area for  working the techniques of controlling  the filaria mosquitoes and the paleria  worms  the health education section of the  filariasis unit has prepared pamphlets  and posters for educating the public  concerning the nature and cause of  filariasis and how the public can  cooperate to help control the disease  lectures conferences and discussions are  held just before the night blood survey  teams visit a valeria area  after the health educators have paved  the way the night blood survey teams go  from house to house in the palarial  villages and take a blood smear on each  member of every family  this provides important basic  information as to the number of persons  having young palarial worms in their  blood  in the interior of british guiana the  native indians have two other types of  filariasis which do not result in  swellings  blood surveys among these indians can be  held in the day because the microfilaria  are present in the blood at all times  the blood smears are taken to the  parasitology laboratory where they are  stained so that the worm-like  microfilaria can be easily seen  each morning entomology field teams  spend the first two working hours in  collecting adult and aquatic stages of  mosquitoes  all kinds of mosquitoes are collected in  order to determine how many different  kinds are carrying the filarial worms  inside houses filaria mosquitoes are  found mostly on walls and on hanging  objects such as clothing curtains and  mosquito nets  they are also found on or under  furniture and in empty containers or  receptacles  the mosquitoes are sucked into a  collecting tube and transferred to cages  another type of filaria mosquito is  usually found in bushy areas  in the coastal trains mosquitoes can  easily be transported for many miles  in british guiana drums containing water  are a second source of breeding of the  main filaria mosquito which is called  culex  the filaria mosquito also breeds in  large tanks some of which are  inaccessible for control work  in this country the main filaria  mosquito breeds in drains only when they  have become contaminated with waste  the female culex mosquito lays its eggs  on the surface of the water they are  cemented together to form a small raft  about one eighth of an inch long  a single dip into a latrine will often  yield hundreds of worm-like larvae and  comma-shaped pupia of culex the adult  mosquito emerges from the pupa dries  itself and flies away  it usually takes only seven to ten days  for the complete development from the  egg to the adult  the water stages of the second kind of  filaria mosquito which is called  mansonia are found associated with  certain water plants especially the  water lettuce  the man sonia larvae and pupae cannot  breathe the air at the surface of the  water like culex  instead they attach themselves to roots  and leaves of water plants which have  air cavities and suck up the oxygen  which they need  the larvae feed while hanging from their  place of attachment  the culex mosquito can be controlled in  two ways  first pit latrines are sprayed with gas  oil  only one such spraying is needed each  month to prevent breeding of the culex  mosquito  each time the lactrine is sprayed it is  marked with paint this provides a record  of the number of times the laptine has  been sprayed  the easiest way to control valeria  mosquito breeding in drums is to empty  them and keep them turned upside down  when water is not being stored  if it is necessary to store up water a  tight fitting drum cover should be made  of wood or metal it should be tightly  replaced after each time that water is  drawn from the drum  the cover should be solid if the drum is  not kept under a drain pipe  if it is kept under a pipe the hole in  the center of the cover should be  covered with a fine screen in order to  prevent the mosquito from laying its  eggs on the water  there should be no gap or hole  whatsoever in the fitted cover  sleeping under a mosquito net will  prevent the mosquito from sucking  infected blood or depositing malaria  worms on the skin  the most important means of controlling  filariasis is by giving out a drug  called genocide or heterozyme  when taken is directed this drug kills  the microfilarii in the blood  in british guiana a single course of  treatment is taken over a period of  seven days in gradually increasing  dosages  after the first day while the drug is  acting on the  the person may not feel well in spite of  this he should continue with the full  treatment because the illness indicates  that the drug is killing the worms in  the blood  after the very active microfilaria are  killed the blood becomes free of filaria  worms the valeria mosquitoes cannot then  pick up the worms while feeding on this  person  the control of malaria cannot be done in  a short time  it involves much cooperation between the  public and health personnel  if each person who is given the drug  takes it in full without fail then  elephantiasis will become a thing of the  past  the worms in the blood will be killed  and the mosquito will not be able to  pass them on to another person when the  night blood collecting teams come to  your house cooperate with them and let  them take blood from your fingers  don't let yourself become deprived of  the use of your legs you can avoid  becoming crippled by valeria  take the advice of the health  authorities who have been doing their  utmost to help eliminate this crippling  scourge  you

